{
  "id": "search-insert-position",
  "questionId": "search-insert-position",
  "slug": "search-insert-position",
  "title": "Search Insert Position",
  "difficulty": "Easy",
  "category": "Binary search",
  "patterns": [
    "binary search"
  ],
  "lists": [
    "interview-150"
  ],
  "hints": [
    "the array is sorted, so 'where would target go' is really asking for the first index whose value is not less than target",
    "binary search doesn't only work for exact matches - it can also narrow down to a boundary between 'too small' and 'big enough'",
    "keep left and right pointers over the valid insertion range; when nums[mid] < target, the answer must be to the right of mid, otherwise it's at mid or to the left",
    "when left and right cross, left is your answer - it works whether or not target is actually present"
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "treating it as a plain exact-match search and returning -1 when target isn't found, instead of the insertion index",
    "off-by-one when the loop ends, returning right instead of left (or vice versa) without checking which one is correct",
    "not handling target being larger than every element, which should return len(nums)"
  ],
  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "You're given an array of distinct integers sorted in ascending order, and\na target value.\n\n**If the target exists in the array, return its index. If it doesn't, return the index where it would need to be inserted to keep the array sorted.**\n",
    "constraints": [
      "1 <= nums.length <= 10^4",
      "-10^4 <= nums[i] <= 10^4",
      "nums contains distinct values sorted in ascending order",
      "-10^4 <= target <= 10^4"
    ],
    "notes": [
      "target smaller than every element (insert at index 0)",
      "target larger than every element (insert at the end)",
      "target equal to an existing element"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Arguments passed into solution function",
    "outputFormat": "Return output matching method signature",
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
